# Architectural Decision Documentation

<introduction>
This reference documents the requirements for clearly documenting architectural decisions that deviate from established patterns in the codebase. It provides guidance for when and how to communicate these decisions to maintain code clarity and consistency.
</introduction>

<context>
- Implementing new features or components that use different approaches
- Introducing alternative architectural patterns
- Optimizing code with non-standard techniques
- Making memory, performance, or resource utilization trade-offs
</context>

<specifications>
<documentation_requirements>
- Document ALL architectural decisions that deviate from established patterns
- Explain WHY the deviation is necessary (technical constraints, performance, etc.)
- Document HOW the deviation works differently from standard patterns
- Include trade-offs and potential future considerations
- Place documentation in BOTH header files (overview) and implementation files (details)
- Use clear, standardized comment blocks with consistent formatting
- Reference related patterns elsewhere in the codebase for comparison
</documentation_requirements>

<communication_protocol>
- Proactively communicate deviations BEFORE implementation
- Document design decisions in technical specifications
- Seek approval for significant pattern changes
- Include justification for why standard patterns are insufficient
- Identify potential maintenance implications
- Tag architectural deviations with standardized comment markers for searchability
</communication_protocol>
</specifications>

<examples>
<example>
// Good documentation for architectural deviation
/**
 * This class uses lazy initialization for datums rather than upfront creation.
 *
 * IMPLEMENTATION NOTE:
 * This deviates from the standard pattern seen in DevicePath/Machine classes:
 * 1. Standard pattern: All datums created during initialization
 * 2. This pattern: Datums created on-demand via getOrCreateDatum()
 *
 * Reasons for deviation:
 * - Potentially hundreds of datums would be wasteful to create upfront
 * - Only a small subset are accessed in any given cycle
 * - Memory usage optimization critical for large installations
 * - Allows prioritizing active tools in scanning logic
 *
 * Trade-offs:
 * - Less consistent with rest of codebase
 * - Slightly more complex datum management
 * - More difficult to track all possible datums
 */
</example>

<example type="alternative">
// Insufficient documentation
// This uses a map instead of member variables for tool offsets
class ToolOffset {
private:
    std::map<std::string, DeviceDatum*> mToolOffsetData;
};
</example>
</examples>

<critical_points>
- ALWAYS document architectural deviations in BOTH header and implementation files
- EXPLAIN why standard patterns were not suitable for this use case
- DOCUMENT all trade-offs and potential maintenance implications
- COMMUNICATE deviations BEFORE implementation rather than after discovery
- IDENTIFY who approved the architectural deviation and when
- DESCRIBE both the standard pattern and how the new pattern differs
- REFERENCE similar patterns elsewhere if they exist
- USE standardized documentation format for consistency
- INCLUDE performance or resource considerations that motivated the deviation
- MAINTAIN documentation when the implementation changes
</critical_points>